    "content" : "Figure 16: Estimated quantile curves"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 225.699, 540.001, 256.337 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "the monotonized quantile curves for Y0 and Y1, respectively. The marginal distribution functions of Y0 and Y1 are obtained by inverting the monotonized quantile curves."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 126.798, 540.003, 216.479 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Table 5 presents estimates of quantiles for potential outcomes and QTE. One noticeable observation is that for SCCG women, low quantiles (q < 0.5) of birth weight from smokers are remarkably higher compared to those for the entire sample or other subgroups, while their nonsmokers’ birth weight quantiles are similar to those in other groups. This leads to the lower quantile smoking eﬀects for this college education group compared to other groups at low quantiles."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 86.193, 540.0, 116.832 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "I also obtain the proportion of potential low birth weight infants to smokers and nonsmokers, F0 (2,500) and F1 (2,500), respectively. As shown in Table 6, 6.5% of babies to smokers would have low birth weight,"
  }, {
